1.1 Dual-Engine Forensic RAG-Diagnostics Orchestrator
The orchestration logic is bifurcated into two specialized execution layers:
* Fast-Path Heuristic Engine (Vercel Edge Runtime): Handles low-latency, deterministic triage queries, symptom code matching, and immediate signal classification. Running on Vercel Edge Functions across global Point of Presence (PoP) locations, this engine evaluates incoming telemetry streams and basic symptom inputs in under 50 milliseconds.
* Deep Forensic RAG Engine (Vercel Serverless Node.js Runtime with Fluid Compute): Handles complex multi-rail power failures, component datasheet context retrieval, and multi-step diagnostic reasoning. Configured with extended function execution limits (maxDuration = 300), it uses Vercel's Fluid Compute model to prevent cold-start penalties and maintain open Server-Sent Events (SSE) connections during long-running Chain-of-Verification loops.
1.2 Multi-Phase Diagnostic Execution Pipeline
The end-to-end triage process runs across four discrete phases:
1. Ingestion & Telemetry Parsing Phase: Real-time ammeter, multimeter, and thermal camera signals are captured via client-side WebUSB, aggregated into 100ms frames, and pushed over Server-Sent Events or WebSockets to the Vercel backend.
2. Symptom-to-Circuit Mapping Phase: Reported failure symptoms (e.g., "MacBook Pro M2 - No Power, 5V 0.02A draw") are cross-referenced against static board netlists and historical fault graph databases to map symptoms to targeted power rails (e.g., PPBUS_G3H, PP3V3_S5) and integrated circuits (ICs).
3. Forensic Retrieval & CoVe Verification Phase: Semantic and hybrid keyword retrieval extracts exact schematics, boardview coordinates, and pinout specs. The 4-stage Chain-of-Verification pipeline runs iteratively to validate diagnostic hypothesis before outputting repair steps.
4. Dynamic Schematic & Rework Rendering Phase: The verified failure node is mapped onto interactive SVG vector boardviews, highlighting shorted nets, target injection points, and expected diode-mode reference ranges for technician execution.
2. Live Hardware Telemetry Streaming Engine
To integrate benchtop testing hardware (power supplies, digital multimeters, electronic loads) into the serverless web environment, Triage AI Rev 4 implements a hybrid browser-edge streaming protocol.
2.1 WebUSB Bridge Architecture
The primary hardware interface operates locally within the technician's browser via the WebUSB API, removing the need for local background agent installation.
* Device Discovery & Filtering: Browser connects directly to USB vendor/product IDs (e.g., Otii Arc, Volteq, Keithley, or custom STM32 USB bridge boards) using standard USB bulk endpoints.
* Web Worker Frame Aggregation: High-frequency raw USB serial data (sampled at 1 kHz) is processed off the main UI thread in a dedicated Web Worker. The worker aggregates voltage (V), current (I), and thermal telemetry into 100ms structured frames.
2.2 Serverless Telemetry Ingestion & Ammeter Signal Parsing
Because Vercel serverless functions are stateless, live streaming telemetry cannot rely on persistent long-lived server sockets hosted on the function instance itself.
* Ingestion Pipeline: Web Workers dispatch telemetry frames to a Vercel Edge Route Handler (app/api/telemetry/stream/route.ts).
* Pub/Sub Routing: The Edge Handler relays frames to Upstash Redis Pub/Sub. Active diagnostic UI sessions subscribe to the Redis channel via Server-Sent Events (SSE) managed by Vercel AI SDK data streams.
* Power-On Waveform Analysis: Ammeter signals are analyzed for characteristic power state signatures. For example, during a boot sequence, current draw transitions are checked against reference power state profiles:
   * S_5 State (Off/Standby): I < 10\text{ mA}
   * S_3 State (Sleep/RAM Refreshed): 100\text{ mA} \le I \le 300\text{ mA}
   * S_0 State (Full Operational Power): I > 1.2\text{ A}
An abrupt current lock at 5V 0.00A indicates an unconsumed primary power rail, triggering an automatic RAG lookup for primary PMIC enable inputs.
2.3 Automated Thermal & Electrical Safety Lockout
To protect delicate motherboard traces from catastrophic thermal runaway during voltage injection, a real-time safety guard loop operates client-side with server logging:
* Client-Side Fast Lockout: The local Web Worker continuously evaluates current thresholds (I_{\text{max}}) and rate-of-change limits (\frac{dI}{dt}). If current spikes above 2.5\text{ A} within \Delta t < 10\text{ ms}, the Web Worker issues an immediate hardware halt command directly over the WebUSB pipe, achieving sub-10ms shutdown latency.
* Audit Logging: Simultaneously, an event snapshot is sent via a Next.js Server Action to Vercel Postgres to record the trip condition for quality assurance and compliance tracking.
3. Interactive SVG Motherboard Schematic Engine
The dynamic vector display engine allows technicians to inspect, search, and run fault injection simulations directly within the web application interface.
3.1 CAD Vector Conversion & Storage Pipeline
Motherboard schematics and boardview files (.brd, .fz, .cad) are converted into vector graphics via an automated build-time and runtime pipeline:
* Layer Segmentation: CAD files are parsed into distinct SVG group structures (<g id="layer-traces">, <g id="layer-components">, <g id="layer-nets">).
* Vercel Blob Storage: Generated SVG assets and binary boardview files are stored in Vercel Blob.
* Vercel Edge Network Caching: SVG assets are delivered via Vercel's global CDN with aggressive cache headers (Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable), ensuring sub-20ms vector rendering anywhere in the world.
3.2 Searchable Text Layer & Component OCR Embedding
To allow programmatic manipulation by the RAG engine, SVG elements are structured with custom semantic HTML5 data attributes:
* Each component pad contains attributes: data-ref="U7100", data-pin="24", data-net="PPBUS_G3H".
* Search text layers undergo OCR spatial normalization during conversion, embedding vector text nodes with explicit coordinate bounds (x, y, w, h).
* When the RAG model identifies a fault on U7100 pin 24, the frontend uses DOM selection (document.querySelector('[data-ref="U7100"][data-pin="24"]')) to automatically pan, zoom, and highlight the component pad.
3.3 Diode Mode Tolerance & Reference Layers
The schematic viewer overlays comparative diagnostic measurements directly onto component pins:
* Diode Mode Measurement: Red multimeter probe on ground, black probe on test point.
* Tolerance Calculation: The system compares measured voltage drop (V_{\text{meas}}) against baseline reference voltage (V_{\text{ref}}) using the relative error formula:
\Delta V_{\text{rel}} = \frac{\vert{}V_{\text{meas}} - V_{\text{ref}}\vert{}}{V_{\text{ref}}}
* Dynamic Visual Overlay Classification:
   * Normal: \Delta V_{\text{rel}} \le 0.05 (Rendered in Green)
   * Drift/Degraded: 0.05 < \Delta V_{\text{rel}} \le 0.15 (Rendered in Amber)
   * Short to Ground / Open Circuit: \Delta V_{\text{rel}} > 0.15 or V_{\text{meas}} \le 0.005\text{ V} (Rendered in Red)
3.4 Fault Injection Simulation Routines
Technicians can simulate voltage injection testing directly on the virtual boardview:
* Circuit Graph Model: Board netlists are loaded into an in-memory graph representation stored in Vercel KV for active sessions.
* Propagation Simulation: Injecting 1.0\text{ V} on PP3V3_S0 runs a breath-first search (BFS) graph traversal through low-resistance paths, highlighting all connected capacitors, IC power pins, and thermal risk zones prior to physical bench injection.
4. Chain-of-Verification (CoVe) Pipeline & RAG Fidelity Engineering
In hardware repair, an incorrect voltage recommendation or wrong pin assignment can permanently destroy a client's CPU or SoC. Triage AI Rev 4 strictly enforces factual accuracy through a 4-stage Chain-of-Verification loop built into the Vercel AI SDK execution pipeline.
4.1 Four-Stage CoVe Verification Loop
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │ Stage 1: Primary Diagnostic Draft                                         │
 │ Generate initial repair path hypothesis based on symptoms & telemetry    │
 └─────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘
                                       │
                                       ▼
 ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │ Stage 2: Verification Query Formulation                                  │
 │ Autonomously extract explicit factual claims (voltages, pins, chips)     │
 └─────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘
                                       │
                                       ▼
 ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │ Stage 3: Isolated Grounding Retrieval                                    │
 │ Issue targeted vector/hybrid queries to verify extracted factual claims  │
 └─────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘
                                       │
                                       ▼
 ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │ Stage 4: Verified Synthesis & Strict Filtering                           │
 │ Re-synthesize output; excise unverified claims; apply refusal if uncertain│
 └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

1. Draft Generation: The primary LLM generates an initial diagnostic strategy based on telemetry and user reports (e.g., "Replace U7100 due to shorted ISL6259 pin 27").
2. Verification Query Formulation: The system parses the draft response and extracts explicit technical claims into a structured verification plan using Zod schema validation:
   * Claim 1: "Is pin 27 of U7100 connected to CSOP?"
   * Claim 2: "Is nominal voltage on CSOP equal to 12.6V?"
3. Isolated Fact Retrieval: Independent retrieval requests execute against Vercel Postgres (pgvector) using dense vector embeddings combined with BM25 sparse text keyword search.
4. Final Verified Synthesis: The model compares the original draft against retrieved canonical schematic data. Any unverified claim is stripped, and the final response is generated.
4.2 Paragraph-Level Keyword Auditing & Strict Abstention Protocols
* Keyword Guard: Generated text passes through an automated regex audit layer that scans for voltage values, netnames, and pin numbers. Every identified netname must exist within the active board's verified netlist database.
* Abstention Protocol: If the similarity score of the top-retrieved schematic chunk drops below the confidence threshold (S_{\text{sim}} < 0.82), the engine triggers an immediate refusal state:
   * Output: "INSUFFICIENT_SCHEMATIC_CONTEXT: Pinout specifications for U7100 cannot be verified with 100% confidence. Please refer to manual physical measurement."
4.3 Context Refinement Fallout Mechanisms
When vector search returns ambiguous or conflicting results across motherboard board revisions (e.g., 820-00165-A vs 820-00165-B), the system activates fallout mechanisms:
* Revision Scoping: Prompts the technician to confirm the physical 820-series PCB silk-screen number.
* Deterministic Lookup Fallback: Bypasses semantic vector search entirely and queries explicit key-value diode-mode tables stored in Vercel KV for deterministic pinout verification.
4.4 Vercel AI SDK Integration Pattern
The CoVe pipeline utilizes Next.js App Router Route Handlers with the Vercel AI SDK (streamText, generateObject):
// app/api/diagnose/route.ts
import { streamText, generateObject } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';
import { z } from 'zod';

export const maxDuration = 300; // Fluid Compute max execution

export async function POST(req: Request) {
 const { symptoms, telemetry, boardId } = await req.json();
 
 // Phase 1: Draft Hypothesis & Verification Plan
 const verificationPlan = await generateObject({
   model: openai('gpt-4o'),
   schema: z.object({
     claims: z.array(z.object({
       component: z.string(),
       pin: z.number().optional(),
       expectedVoltage: z.string().optional(),
       query: z.string(),
     })),
   }),
   prompt: `Analyze symptoms for board ${boardId}: ${symptoms}. Formulate factual verification claims.`,
 });

 // Phase 2: Execute Hybrid Vector Retrieval (pgvector)
 // ... [Retrieval logic executed across Vercel Postgres]

 // Phase 3: Final Streamed Output over SSE
 const result = streamText({
   model: openai('gpt-4o'),
   system: 'You are Triage AI Rev 4 Engine. Output strictly verified technical diagnostics.',
   prompt: `Synthesize verified repair path using context...`,
 });

 return result.toDataStreamResponse();
}

